Greetings from the land of the midnight gun^H^H^Hsun...

	Living in Canada, where gun controls have been a part of everyday
life for many years, I'm positively green with envy at the casual discussion
about acquiring and firing various types of handguns.  I've fired exactly 
three different handguns in my life...

	A Browning 9mm (Canadian Armed Forces standard handgun from perhaps
1940 until quite recently).  An absolute pig of a weapon...but I'm probably
biased because I couldn't hit the side of a hill with one...

	A Ruger .22 single-shot target pistol.  Very nice, but .22 just 
doesn't do anything for me...I was spoiled by having too many "free"
military rounds to fire during my Reserve service.

	A Smith & Wesson .357, with (I was told) a custom trigger assembly.
This was a dream to fire...every squeeze of the trigger was just that - a
squeeze, not a jerk or a strain or a pull, just take up the pressure
gradually and the round went off as a slight surprise.  I've never shot any
weapon that felt so [words fail me] wonderful.  I only fired 20 rounds from
this pistol, but I'd have paid mucho dinero on the spot for one...had it
been legal in Canada to do so...but it wasn't.

	In order to get a handgun in Canada, I'd have to get a Firearms
Acquisition Certificate (FAC), which is a federal "gun owner's permit".
This is issued through local/regional police departments and requires some
background checking by the RCMP.  I'd also have required a Restricted
Weapons Permit, which is only available to certain people - members of
police departments, members of registered gun clubs, people deemed to have
a "need" for a restricted weapon.  This last category of folks might include
people who have received "credible" death threats...but I'm not sure of
that.

	The obvious way to be entitled to such a permit would be to join
a gun club, right?  Well, most gun clubs have waiting lists that are literally
years long.  And, since they're in a seller's market, they tend to be very
expensive (hundreds to thousands of dollars per year, plus some kind of
initiation fee in the same range).

	Even having passed these hurdles, I still would have to get
a permit to carry the weapon in an approved container from the store to
my home (by a designated route), and then a second permit to allow me
to take the gun from my home to the gun club (with no deviation from the
approved route).  I'd also need to have a law enforcement person inspect my 
gun safe or other lockable storage location in my home.

	The above paragraph is getting less definite, because I never did
go through all the rest of the paperwork (and monetary outlay) after getting
my FAC.  I understand that the latest rules on restricted firearms are much
tougher about storage and transportation requirements than the ones I was
familiar with, but perhaps someone else can fill in the details.

	I'm telling you all this, because it's where the U.S. will be
heading on gun control as fast as the current administration can drive it.
Welcome to a kinder, gentler, less safe world.  But, then again, maybe I'm
just too damned cynical...


