Date: Tue, 14 Dec 1993 03:10:38 -0800
From: mlshew@netcom.com (Mark Shewmaker)
Subject: Transhuman Language (was Speed Reading/Speed Writing?)

James Baker writes:

>   In addition to faster communication (more bandwidth) what other
> properties should the successor to "natural language" have?  For example:
>
> Thread control....a "tag" or "stub" could be added at conversation points that
>                   might be worth coming back to

This is very useful even in mundane conversations today, especially long
rambling telephone calls with a friend when you're in front of a computer,
or have pen and paper I guess.  (Or a Newton?)

Imagine keeping track of your threads with a simple stack.

Keep a simple text editor in front of you.

You're talking about "A", and one of you mentions "B", which is an
offshoot topic you'll probably discuss for a while.  Type "A" in the
editor, and hit return.  If you offshoot from "B", then type "B",
and hit return.  As any topic dies off, bring back and start discussing
the last item on the stack written in front of you, then delete it when
you're done.

It is absolutely amazing how effective such an absurdly simple
technique can be.  We all think we'll "just remember" topics to
come back to, but I often don't unless I actually write it down.

Impress your friends!  (Really!  When I've done this on the phone
for the first time while talking to different friends, they have
invariably been amazed that we got back to everything, and complimented
me on "remembering" multiple times in the conversation.)  It's
amazing to me that even in casual conversations that we can take
"forgetting" for granted to such an extent!

Of course, adding a second stack of your own topics-list, perhaps
with a multiwindow editor, adds even more functionality to the
scheme, but still keeping it mind-boggingly simple.

