I enjoyed the descriptions of memory-expanding methods, speedreading,
and speedtalking, as well as the discussions of the ramifications
of such things.  I'd recommend the story to any extropian.  (Especially
if you enjoy Heinlein-style spy thrillers, and somehow haven't gotten
around to this one.)


And for the benefit of any who haven't read up on speedreading,
and even some who have, I also _strongly_ recommend:

     _The Evelyn Wood 7-Day Speed Reading and Learning Program_,
     by Stanly Frank, ISBN 0-388-71577-5, paperback, $4.95 (?)

Just force yourself to read though the book in order, and don't
try to out-think the methods.  That is, don't over-rationalize
the situation, convincing yourself that _you_ don't need to do
the things suggested; just do the silly exercises.

I've seen the book at chain bookstores, Targets, and K-Marts,
so you shouldn't have much difficulty finding it.  As a $5
paperback, it's not likely to break many people's budgets.
And, it's written at a fifth grade reading level, so no-one
should have difficulty understanding it.  (No post-graduate
work required in the fields of economics, game theory,
number theory, complexity theory, cryptography, medicine,
or philosophy.)

In many ways, it's quite unlike most books recommended here.  :-)

As you might have guessed, this book is not a psychological
treatise.  Rather, it's more of a very elementary handholding
tutorial.  If you've learned about speedreading before, you're
likely not to learn much factual information, but I'd still
recommend it, mostly because this book isn't a psychological
proof of concept for increasing information flow from book to
brain, but more of a how-to book describing these methods in a
usable form.  (I'd especially recommend the book to people who've
heard that speedreading means you "simply skip the nonessential
words."  Nonsense.)

The techniques really do work, but you do have to consciously
maintain your improved reading speed.  (Or not, if you get
lax with it, as I have.)

(Hold off on those other technical books; buy this and read your
_Intro to Kolmogorov Complexity_ while just browsing in the store!
It's a lot cheaper.  And you though the actions of the ILF would
have ramifications throughout the publishing industry.  HA!)

:-)

He also talks about efficient note-taking methods, which are useful
for the speed-reading technique, note-taking for classes, lectures,
and composing, and memory enhancement.

(Embarrassingly, I've forgotten to what extent this is covered--I've
lent my copy out, and I've got this info filed in my mind in the same
place as a mostly-read-in-the-bookstore pop psychology speedreading
book by someone who's name begins with the letter 'B'.  [Remembered
in proofreading--Tony Buzan.  Anyone heard of him?  He wrote about
increasing memory recall and "Mind Mapping".]  I've got to remember
to look that book back up next time I go to that bookstore--it had
pointers in its bibliography about people using similar techniques
to vastly increase their speed, absorption, and understanding of
mathematical concepts, in addition to discussions of more mundane
arithmetic skill improvements.  Since that's more related to the
original question Harvey asked, I'll make a point to look it up next
time I go.)

Now, for the people who stubbornly don't want to buy or skim through
the book, and are willing to take the risk that my explanations might
ruin their entire future chances at self-transformation, minor spoilers
as to the theory and method (from memory) follow:

(You've likely heard quite a bit of it before.)



0.  Posture, setting, and lighting are important.  Sit up, in a
    well-lit area, with the book flat on the table, with no
    distractions, and your mind on the subject.

    Don't try to speed-read slumped sideways in a chair, with your
    page-turning speed limited by your position.

1.  A person can only speak so fast.  You don't want your reading
    speed limited by your speaking speed.

2.  A person can only understand spoken speech so fast.  You wouldn't
    want your reading limited by the speed at which you can understand
    sped-up speech.

3.  Most people, when reading, "subvocalize" the words.  That is, they
    actually pronounce them to themselves in their mind.  (And not only
    are there people who mumble to themselves, many people almost-mumble,
    just barely controlling their vocal cords and twitching their lips.)

    Even the completely "in-the-mind" subvocalization puts a _major_ dent
    in reading speed.  This narration must be avoided at all costs!

4.  One useful exercise:  Get a easy-to-read fiction book, or some
    book without any math or symbols.  Figure out how fast you can
    read it, roughly:  Read through a page or three, following along
    at a mostly constant speed with your finger.  Try to keep your finger
    at the same speed, and read faster or slower so as to keep up with
    it.

    Then go back and do it again, except, pronounce the first word of
    the first line, while you continue reading to yourself.  When
    you finish speaking that word, start speaking the word your finger
    happens to be on at the time, and continue reading the text while
    you are speaking that word.  Do this for ten minutes or so.

    The idea here is to help your mind uncouple the process of reading
    and pronouncing.

5.  Another exercise:  For however fast you naturally read, tested
    while you read with your finger following along, force yourself
    to read twice as fast, following your finger left to right over
    each line.  Don't worry about comprehension, just let your eyes
    pass over those words at that speed or greater.

    You'll amaze yourself by absorbing a greater and greater percentage.

    There are a lot of extreme variations on this exercise--reading
    for comprehension, reading for speed, and for any of the five times
    his method suggests you read a book.  I'm not going to go into
    that here, except to say that a mere doubling of your base reading
    speed is simply the very beginning.  (The fives time involve different
    types of very fast pre-scanning.)

    The amazing thing here is that your brain can comprehend a lot more
    than you might think.  If you force yourself to read faster than the
    artificial limit you've set for it, you'll start to see how much
    you've been limiting yourself.

    (In elementary school, after a while we are taught to refrain from
    keeping track of our place in the text with our fingers.  Our teachers
    said it was bad to keep doing this.  They used to say that left-handers
    were evil or something.  Both theories were wrong.

    (Perhaps Duncan Frissell could add in one of his nifty anecdotes here.))

6.  Do the same exercise as above, with your finger leading your eye,
    reading sans narration, line-by-line, except read one line
    left-to-right, and the next line right-to-left, saving on those
    carriage returns.

    You'll find that your brain will sort it all out for you, and you'll
    understand everything perfectly.

7.  The next step (if I remember correctly), is to simultaneously read
    two lines left-to-right, and two right-to left.

    Again, your brain will sort it out.

8.  (I'm skipping a lot of stuff, you understand--buy the book.)

    Then try different patterns to follow your finger through the
    page, tracing a kind of repeated X, or a zig-zag pattern, or
    repeated lying down, continuous half-8's, or perhaps two parallel
    vertical lines per page.

    Those two parallel lines can be followed simultaneously, or one
    after the other.  That is, you can read down the left half of the
    page, and up the right half, and repeat for the other page, but
    it is usually fastest to read down one page and then up the other,
    as there's less hand movement and it makes page-turning much more
    efficient (less clumsy).

    And yes, your brain _can_ sort it out.  Trust me.

