>but that didn't quite work out.  


The Newton can't read shorthand yet. but there is nothing inherent in
the OS to keep someone from building recognizers for shorthand (in fact,
one of our product concepts involves building a new set of recognizers
for musical notation). Although with the current OS a programmer can't
actually futz with the character set or the recognizers, this will change
by next year (and you can do shape interpretation now, so if you wanted,
you could build an alternative set of gestures for proofing Newton text).

>(I realize Newtons do recognition by words, and not characters.)

A Newton does *not* recognize by "word", except in that it has built-in
and user dictionaries which it can check against to come up with a set
of best guesses for what you've written. But if you want, you can tell
the Newton to simply try to interpret the characters you write. My Newton
and I are just starting to get to the point were I can actually turn this
option on and the device does a good enough job that it knows that I'm
writing "computer" and not "dpnqv+on", but it will now often get brand new
words like "telephony" on the first try, and more probably have the word
listed in the alternative menu (when you write a word on the Pad and it
changes your "ink" to text, you can double-tap on the word and the Newton
will pop up a menu with a list of possible interpreted words, the original
"ink" and a little keyboard icon which allows you to type using the virtual
keyboard). 


>I still think it's a neat idea.  Anyone think it might be a feature of
>future Newtons?

Count on it by the end of 94.

 - jeff

