Date: Tue, 14 Dec 1993 10:21:29 -0800 (PST)
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Newton and Synecdoche

Harry Shapiro writes:

> A company is going to sell a "back plate" for the Sharp version
> of the MessagePad (what you folks call the Newton).

I admit to constantly calling my Apple Newton MessagePad simply "my
Newton." Newton is of course the overall name of the technology, as in
"Newton Slate," "Newton MessagePad," "Newton PostItPad," etc.

Since there is currently only one instance of the technology, the
MessagePad, there is no ambiguity.

Using the more general class name for a specific instance is sort of a
variant on synechdoche (pronounced "sin-ek-doe-key"), the device of
naming something after a _part_, as in:

- "He's got a great set of wheels." (wheels = car)

- "Put the transistor up to your ear." (transistor = radio)

- "Ten sails on the horizon." (sails = saiboats)

- "Another Jupiter just joined the list." (Jupiter = Jupiter-sized
brain = a new list member)

Syndecdoche is a common abstraction and I often wish my education had
been more classical, so I'd know and appreciate more such
terms. Computer terms are easy to pick up, but having a deep
understanding of metonymy and meter takes early indoctrination.

--Tim May


