From: Elizabeth Schwartz <betsys@cs.umb.edu>
Subject: Human Language (was The Importance of Reading)

The first human writings were pictorial, more like diagrams. A picture of
a bear and a cave, a picture of deer running and arrows, etc.

  It took quite a few levels of abstraction to get to today's phonetic 
alphabets. People had to invent the use of written symbols to express
abstract nouns and verbs (they also had to have abstract nouns and verbs,
come to think of it.) Possibly the first steps were using symbols to
represent names. The ancient Egyptions used concrete symbols to represent
homophones, sound-alike words, and then abstracted to using the picture of
an object to represent a sound in that object's name.

 (I minored inlinguistics 15 years ago, so my memory of the details is
hazy) 

