Date: Tue, 14 Dec 93 13:42:30 EST
From: eli@suneast.east.sun.com (Elias Israel - SunSelect Engineering)
Subject: TECH: Continental/PSI Internet Service

OK, here's my report on the Continental/PSI cooperation to give
internet service to cable customers:

I finally got a chance to talk to the Chief Engineer in NH who was
able to answer my technical questions. They are going to roll out this
service at the end of January in Cambridge, MA. Pending the success of
that project, they intend to extend the service to six other MA cities
in 1995 (including Burlington, where I live).

I asked him about the transmission methods and learned that the answer
was "all of the above." He basically has three options. All of the
options stress data going to the home rather than coming away from it
(a reasonable thing to do, considering the uses to which it will be
put, probably). The choices are:

	(1) Bi-directional data flow on the cable, with 10Mbps
	    going to the home and 4Mbps coming from the home.

	(2) 10Mbps to the home on the cable, with 19.2Kbps (or
	    thereabouts) from the home using a modem/phone line
	    combination.

	(3) Bi-directional data flow on the cable, ~500Kpbs in
	    both directions.

He said that the likeliest outcome would be that all of these
different methods would be available and would be priced accordingly.

In addition, different levels of software service would be available
that would give you your own email address, NNTP service, etc. I
said that I assumed that the services would be tailored for a relatively
unsophisticated user, he told me I was incorrect. It's their plan to
create an "open architecture" that would support all of the services
that sophisticated Internet users have come to expect, including
new services like WWW (he specifically mentioned Mosaic and Viola).

Overall, I definitely got the impression that these folks intend to
do this thing right and they certainly seem to have a clue.

I had asked Henry James where all of the bandwidth for this service
would come from, and he gave me some interesting responses. I told 
him that it was my understanding that each cable "head" serves somewhere
between 2000 and 5000 homes. He said that these days, they serve many
more than that. Apparently, in Long Island, NY, they have a single head
that services about half a million homes! Closer to home, a single
head in western MA serves more than 200,000 homes.

James explained to me that the cables in any particular locale have any
number of channels that can't be used because the "ingress" (crosstalk
between cable signal and local TV transmissions) would be too great.
Consequently, they have a certain amount of their cable assets tied up
in bandwidth they can't really use. With the addition of digital technologies
(including error correction, signal segmentation and reassembly, etc.) it
is now possible for them to recapture those assets. Another wonderful
example of how increasing levels of technology *create* whole new
resources that were previously useless. In fact, they're already using
this kind of technology to send cable radio signals.

Nevertheless, even with the added bandwidth, they're counting on packets
from homes to be somewhat "bursty" and relatively infrequent in comparison
with data flowing to the home.

I hope this clears up the questions. I'm certainly looking forward to
this service in my area. One final note: Continental Cablevision
customers who want to learn about when the service might be coming
to their area can call PSI at 1-800-82PSI82.

